%I A398450 #25 Aug 05 2026 19:09:02
%S A398450 4,6,27,30,45,150,180,184,245,260,294,374,414,510,848,1014,1323,4002,
%T A398450 5406,15424,21645,22644,24610,29673,35310,193152,891310,1037824,
%U A398450 2468151,4095712,4170752,4943106,10321120,12228390,16726016,16791696,17409376,17981920,50251776,85686032,87130720
%N A398450 Composite numbers k such that phi(k) divides sigma(k) - tau(k).
%C A398450 Here phi(k) is Euler's totient function, sigma(k) is the sum of the positive divisors of k, and tau(k) is the number of positive divisors of k.
%C A398450 Every prime p trivially satisfies the divisibility condition, since sigma(p) - tau(p) = (p + 1) - 2 = p - 1 = phi(p).
%C A398450 Therefore only composite solutions are included.
%C A398450 The terms appear sparse: there are 18 terms below 5000 and 29 terms below 3000000.
%C A398450 If p = 2^k - 2*k + 1 is prime for k > 0 (A301744), then 2^(k-2) * p is a term. - _Amiram Eldar_, Jul 30 2026
%D A398450 Tom M. Apostol, Introduction to Analytic Number Theory, Springer-Verlag, 1976, Chapter 2.
%e A398450 1323 = 3^3 * 7^2 is a term because sigma(1323) = 2280, tau(1323) = 12, and phi(1323) = 756. Hence (sigma(1323) - tau(1323))/phi(1323) = (2280 - 12)/756 = 3.
%t A398450 Select[Range[4, 100000],! PrimeQ[#] &&Divisible[DivisorSigma[1, #] - DivisorSigma[0, #],EulerPhi[#]] &]
%o A398450 (PARI) isok(k) = { if(isprime(k), return(0)); my(f = factor(k)); (sigma(f) - numdiv(f)) % eulerphi(f) == 0; };
%o A398450 forcomposite(k = 4, 10^5, if(isok(k), print1(k, ", ")))
%Y A398450 Cf. A000010 (Euler totient function), A065608 (sigma - tau), A055465, A301744.
%K A398450 nonn,new
%O A398450 1,1
%A A398450 _Lark Song_, Jul 30 2026
%E A398450 More terms from _Michel Marcus_, Jul 30 2026
